@renstein8210 You just proved again that you have a superficial understanding of how global technology works. Saying Taiwanese and Korean chips "aren't Western" is like saying a book belongs to the printing press rather than the author and the machine inventor.


TSMC and Samsung are the foundries, but they are completely trapped by Western IP and technology at every single step of the process. They cannot etch a single modern silicon wafer without EUV lithography machines. These are manufactured by one single company on Earth: ASML in the Netherlands. Furthermore, ASML's machines rely on laser technology from Germany  and optics from Zeiss. On top of that, Taiwan and Korea print chip designs created by American companies using software created entirely by American firms.


Without Western design, Western software, and Dutch-German machinery, Taiwan's factories are just empty, useless buildings. The entire Asian semiconductor industry exists because the West allows it to manufacture their intellectual property. Are you learning how the global supply chain works yet little buddy?
